  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "rh4cGGwkUGfS9VxuAAL1w8y8qZI=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:27:14.475Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The flag on M&I/Partners is unambiguous: not a delivery partner. It directs the firm to be routed to the healthcare vertical via influence instead, and cites one concrete artefact as the reason \u2014 it benchmarks hospital IT spend at 6.0% of revenue.\n\nThe mechanism is its vendor-neutrality, which the record describes as by design rather than incidental. A firm that never carries a build has no platform slot to win and no bench to staff, so it cannot be scored the way an integrator is. What it produces instead is analysis that healthcare buyers use, and a published spend benchmark is the kind of output that a whole sector reads. That is why the research treats it as leverage rather than as pipeline.\n\nTwo caveats bound the claim. The row is a provisional v2 tail entry, rescored mechanically on size and slot occupancy with no v3 evidence, so this is a hypothesis about a channel rather than a qualified relationship. And the priority field still carries an outstanding OSS-portfolio screen, which for an advisory firm is a cheaper check than for a delivery house.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**4 \u00b7 needs OSS screen**\n- M&I/Partners (NL)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> DERIVED, NOT SCORED ON v3 \u2014 v2 row rescored mechanically (size + slot-occupancy only). Run the OSS-portfolio screen before acting. v2 verticals: Healthcare, public \u00b7 NL profile: healthcare, public \u00b7 rev n/a \u00b7 Not a delivery partner. Route to the healthcare vertical via influence: it benchmarks hospital IT spend at 6.0% of revenue.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:27:14.475325Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:27:14.475330Z"
    }
  },
